research='''# Research and Competitive Technology Review\n\n**Review checked:** 2026-10-16. This is an evidence-backed starting review, not a claim that every model was rerun in this clean-room build. Prior machine experiments are labeled historical evidence. Re-benchmark before changing defaults or weights.\n\n## System conclusions\n1. The studio is agent-operated: the reasoning agent directs local commands and creative tools; the core does not require a paid reasoning API.\n2. Optimize **usable integrated assets and shipped experience per unit time**, not raw image count or a single model score.\n3. Asset tasks differ: concept illustration, pixel sprite, transparent icon, environment plate, keyframe, modular tile, UI control and 3D rig have different constraints.\n4. Image identity and animation are distinct. A seed is not identity lock; interpolation does not design a missing action.\n5. Generation, refinement, integration, engine validation, human art review, rights review and release are separate statuses.\n\n## Model/workflow findings from the submitted archive\n\n### FLUX.2 Klein\nThe official Black Forest Labs repo's model table lists Klein 4B and 4B Base as Apache-2.0; 9B variants and FLUX.2 dev use the FLUX Non-Commercial License in that table. It describes text-to-image and single/multi-reference editing. Verify exact downloaded file and current terms at adoption.\n\nPrior local experiment reports Klein 4B distilled FP8 producing detailed character/environment concepts in roughly 8–10 seconds per 1024px candidate and system-observed peak near 11.1/12.2 GB VRAM. This is historical, machine-specific evidence, not a guaranteed benchmark. Keep GPU jobs sequential and remeasure cold/warm time.\n\n### SD 1.5 pixel-art checkpoint\nThe prior workspace repeatedly observed poor front-view control and sprite-sheet outputs needing manual frame picking. It remains suitable only for task categories where its style and control behavior are proven. It is not a universal pose-control system.\n\n### Z-Image Turbo\nPrior single-brief tests reported 512px execution but narrow VRAM headroom and a character readability/pose failure. Sample size is too small to assign a general quality ranking. Keep it experimental until controlled per-category evidence supports promotion; verify exact source and license.\n\n### ComfyUI\nThe adapter uses the local API workflow/prompt/history/view path. Workflow shapes and nodes are version-sensitive; validate nodes, inputs, model IDs and output paths. Queue acceptance does not mean completed generation.\n\n### Animation methods\nChoose among authored frame-by-frame art, reference-conditioned editing, 2D cutout/rigging, Blender rigs rendered to sprites, and interpolation according to the actual character/style. Required proof includes movement and attack at target speed, prop ownership, pivot/alpha correctness, in-engine playback and human visual inspection. The source archive's Klein identity experiment showed promising individual poses but not universal walk-cycle reliability. RIFE was rejected for one sprite test where output became opaque.\n\n## Public professional learning boundary\nPixar in a Box is useful public education on storytelling, animation, light/color, cameras, rigging, effects and environments. Blender and Godot docs provide implementable techniques. This repository does not claim access to private AAA pipelines or reproduce confidential methods. It captures transferable public principles and tests local implementation.\n\n## Source register\nSee `source-register.json` for official source links, type, use and caveats. Key sources:\n- FLUX.2 model matrix: https://github.com/black-forest-labs/flux2\n- ComfyUI docs/API: https://docs.comfy.org/ and https://github.com/Comfy-Org/ComfyUI/blob/master/openapi.yaml\n- Godot sprite animation: https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/2d/2d_sprite_animation.html\n- Godot animation blending: https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/animation/animation_tree.html\n- Godot CLI: https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/editor/command_line_tutorial.html\n- Blender animation: https://www.blender.org/features/animation/\n- RIFE: https://github.com/nihui/rife-ncnn-vulkan\n- Pixar in a Box: https://www.khanacademy.org/computing/pixar\n- MCP tools: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/concepts/tools\n- JSON Schema: https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12\n- GitHub Actions security: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/security/secure-use\n\n## Evidence boundary\n- **Implemented in this clean-room repo:** Python service/package, local adapters, asset/animation utilities, planning/state model, schema contracts and specialist knowledge library; current-rebuild evidence is in `tests/` and `VERIFICATION.md`.\n- **Inherited historical evidence:** Klein/Z-Image generations, RIFE caveat, Godot Ember/Lumen builds, prior 53-test suite and GPU measurements. These were not rerun by this build-container package run.\n- **Not proven by this repository alone:** every model fits every task, universally coherent AI-generated animation, AAA quality, winning jams, or blanket commercial rights.\n'''
(ROOT/'docs'/'research'/'visual-production-competitive-review.md').write_text(research,encoding='utf-8')

arch='''# Architecture and Responsibility Boundaries\n\n## Design objective\nThe studio is a local-first production substrate an external reasoning agent operates. It does not bundle a reasoning model and does not imply an agent has expert judgment merely because it can call tools. Expertise is encoded in skills, contracts, tested utilities and evidence gates.\n\n## Package map\n- `cli.py`: operator-facing CLI; predictable JSON output.\n- `service.py`: application command registry, dependency composition and run tracking.\n- `api/http_server.py`: authenticated loopback HTTP wrapper.\n- `mcp_server.py`: JSON-RPC over stdio for the studio command service.\n- `readiness.py`: read-only production readiness aggregation (tools, loopback services, RAM/GPU/disk, host MCP Blender override).\n- `adapters/`: explicit boundaries for ComfyUI, SD WebUI, Godot, Blender, RIFE and generation workflow builders.\n- `assets/`: image inspection/refinement, animation diagnostics/assembly and atlas packing.\n- `workflow.py` / `planner.py`: lifecycle state and timeboxed production planning.\n- `store.py`: local SQLite run/event/asset/feedback evidence.\n- `schemas/`, `skills/`, `docs/`: production contracts and specialist knowledge.\n- `templates/`: explicitly marked engine smoke fixture, not finished art.\n- `tools/creative_mcp_server.py`: bounded creative-tool MCP server; fixed bundled scripts, workspace-contained paths, `--disable-autoexec` Blender subprocesses.\n- `docs/registries/`: asset-method and MCP-tool registries validated against repository schemas.\n\n## Invariants\n1. The agent chooses creative strategy; adapters perform narrow, explicit operations.\n2. Providers and heavy dependencies are optional; planning works without GPU packages.\n3. Workspace writes use resolved paths, explicit overwrite intent and safe archive handling.\n4. Outputs preserve provenance and separate tool success from art/release approval.\n5. GPU jobs should be serialized when VRAM is constrained.\n6. Human approval gates stay open until a person supplies the decision.\n7. Reproducibility uses versioned configuration, hashes and retained source inputs.\n\n## Extension contract\nA new capability requires a stable interface, input/output contract, dependency and error contract, success/failure tests, capability-register entry and skill/doc references. Keep third-party objects behind adapters. Add schemas backward-compatibly where possible. Write an architecture decision record for dependency direction, persistence, safety, or data-contract changes.\n\n## Not claimed\nThis code does not supply universal semantic matting, automatic art direction, general video generation, a full game engine, private AAA studio techniques, or market-success prediction. It is extensible toward specialized local tools, with evidence-based capability labels.\n'''
(ROOT/'docs'/'architecture.md').write_text(arch,encoding='utf-8')

limits='''# Known Limitations and Capability Boundaries\n\n- **Artistic quality:** current metrics do not certify commercial quality. The agent owns producing and iterating candidate art; inspect important assets in-game and label agent assessment, any explicitly required human review, and rights review as distinct evidence states. Routine art creation is not an operator task.\n- **Identity consistency:** same seed is not identity lock. Historical Klein img2img was promising but had a hand/prop ownership fault; review each action frame.\n- **Animation:** frame assembly/anchor metrics do not judge semantic motion, weight, arcs, timing, foot sliding or expressive readability. Cutout and Blender routes need project-specific verification.\n- **RIFE:** interpolation is not semantic action generation. A historical sprite test produced opaque frames and was rejected.\n- **Background removal:** edge-connected color-distance cleanup is not semantic matting and can erase valid edges if tolerance is high.\n- **VRAM:** source evidence reports near-limit 11.1/12.2 GB during some generation workflows. Historical machine-specific values only; serialize jobs and remeasure when versions change.\n- **External services:** ComfyUI, SD WebUI, Godot, Blender and RIFE are optional. This rebuild does not invoke GPU generation or prove those tools are installed on a new machine.\n- **Godot export:** the adapter checks commands and expected files; actual target-runtime/gameplay tests are separate.\n- **MCP:** protocol tests do not verify every target agent host's configuration.\n- **Jam estimates:** plans use estimated durations and reserve; observed data should calibrate estimates.\n- **Licensing:** registers are evidence tools, not legal advice; check exact weights/assets/fonts/data and redistribution rights.\n- **Public distribution:** model weights, local state, `.env`, virtual environments and repetitive game exports are excluded by default.\n- **Historical evidence:** archive results are not test results for this clean-room package. Current evidence is documented separately.\n'''
(ROOT/'docs'/'known-limitations.md').write_text(limits,encoding='utf-8')

roadmap='''# Roadmap: From Core to Demonstrated Production Depth\n\n## Included in this clean-room base\n- Local-first CLI, authenticated loopback HTTP API and MCP JSON-RPC surface.\n- ComfyUI API and SD WebUI adapters; Klein/Z-Image recipe builders based on prior tested workflows.\n- Godot, Blender and RIFE probes; guarded Godot validation/export.\n- SQLite runs/events/asset/feedback state.\n- Workflow state machine and 24h/72h/14d task planner with DAG cycle checks.\n- Optional image inspection/refinement, contact sheets, deterministic atlas packing and animation diagnostics/assembly.\n- Bounded creative MCP server over stdio (preflight, animation-sequence and pose-spec validation, Blender inventory/authoring/render review) plus a read-only `ags readiness` report.
- Structured JSON Schema contracts, 87 specialist skills, agent-first production contract, animation execution runbook, research register and known-limitations guide.\n\n## Highest-value next steps based on evidence\n1. Improve generation queue job persistence, cancellation, retry and resume.\n2. Add a reference-identity review system with accepted/rejected candidate decisions and explicit human ratings.\n3. Prototype Blender-to-sprite production against reference-guided frame workflows on a common workload.\n4. Keep host-level Freebuff/Claude/MCP integration coverage current as connector versions change.\n5. Extend visual-review tooling for animation playback and in-engine asset comparison.\n6. Add new game templates only when they include playable loop, art direction, audio, tests and reproducible release.\n7. Calibrate jam estimates from real projects/postmortems.\n\n## Explicitly not promised\nThis roadmap does not guarantee AAA quality, industry dominance, market success, or any business valuation. Those depend on execution, player response, design, rights, team capability and market factors that a repository alone cannot determine.\n'''
(ROOT/'docs'/'ROADMAP.md').write_text(roadmap,encoding='utf-8')

# Skill overview for humans; machine index is generated from each SKILL.md.
(ROOT/'docs'/'studio'/'specialist-skill-library.md').write_text('''# Specialist Skill Library\n\nThe authoritative playbooks live in `skills/<skill-id>/SKILL.md`. They use compact frontmatter and standard procedure/acceptance/failure/evidence/handoff sections so compatible agents can retrieve only the skills relevant to the current project.\n\nThe library covers discovery, creative thesis, core loop and systems, action, platformers, roguelites, RPG, tactics, deckbuilders, survival/crafting, puzzles, horror, narrative, racing, simulation, Metroidvania, shooters, multiplayer risk, procedural generation, art direction, pixel/painterly/anime/isometric/low-poly/realistic/voxel/graphic styles, characters, environments, props, image prompting, ComfyUI, model benchmarking, refinement, extraction, upscaling, atlases, animation principles/keyframes/cutout/RIFE/Godot/Blender/full-stack character animation/rigging and retargeting/action design, combat VFX and audio, agent capability discovery, production direction, visual gameplay validation, VFX, lighting, materials, UI, audio, accessibility, level design, game AI, physics, architecture, Python, performance, QA, playtesting, visual review, licensing, security, MCP, release, planning, postmortems, save/load, localization, camera, narrative, content pipelines, agent handoffs, cross-platform export, and evidence standards.\n\nUse `ags skills search <query>` or `ags skills read <skill-id>`. Skill presence means guidance is available; it does not mean every corresponding production capability has been implemented or tested. Use the capability register for that distinction.\n''',encoding='utf-8')

print('Built workflows, research register and architecture docs')
